1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:01,950
Step two was the last step I built.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:03,430
The recipe has six steps.

3
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:07,390
I built step one, then three, then
five, and only weeks later four and six.

4
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:10,790
Step two, the one that runs
second, was written last of all.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,170
So what were the other steps
doing while it was missing?

6
00:00:14,167 --> 00:00:16,797
Tanmay Kulkarni, for Humanitarians AI.

7
00:00:17,147 --> 00:00:18,817
In Mycroft, a public repository,

8
00:00:18,927 --> 00:00:21,517
a recipe is a list of steps
with a script behind each one.

9
00:00:21,807 --> 00:00:24,657
Mine fetches prices for five
stocks and adds them up.

10
00:00:24,747 --> 00:00:27,897
In the recipe I wrote that step
three reads the price files directly,

11
00:00:27,917 --> 00:00:29,297
since step two is still open.

12
00:00:29,347 --> 00:00:33,137
And that step five reads step three's
output, since step four is still open.

13
00:00:33,327 --> 00:00:35,997
Those are my sentences, and when
I wrote them they were true.

14
00:00:36,447 --> 00:00:39,777
Each is a stand-in: a shortcut
for a step that did not exist yet.

15
00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:41,357
So I will put one check to each.

16
00:00:41,377 --> 00:00:43,557
What did it stand in
for? What did it skip?

17
00:00:43,747 --> 00:00:46,877
And what happens when it and the
real step get the same wrong input?

18
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:50,030
This week the real step two was merged.

19
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:53,030
It reads the run settings and the
workflow, then each price file,

20
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:55,290
and checks every file
against the hash recorded in

21
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:57,210
a manifest when the
sample files were made.

22
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:59,390
It keeps each file exactly as it came.

23
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:02,770
A file goes to a reject list if
the manifest does not list it,

24
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:06,010
if its hash has changed, if it is
missing, or if it is not valid JSON.

25
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:08,370
It does not judge whether
a price makes sense.

26
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,350
That stays with steps three and four.

27
00:01:11,267 --> 00:01:12,477
First, the good news.

28
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:16,097
I ran the stand-in and the real step
on the same 6 sets of sample files,

29
00:01:16,117 --> 00:01:18,077
and they saw the same files every time.

30
00:01:18,447 --> 00:01:20,797
In the clean set, 5 files and 5 records.

31
00:01:21,047 --> 00:01:22,917
In the set of deliberately
defective files,

32
00:01:23,047 --> 00:01:24,917
step two took in four and turned one away,

33
00:01:24,937 --> 00:01:28,637
while step three looked at the same 5 and
rejected all of them, for its own reasons.

34
00:01:28,657 --> 00:01:32,377
6 sets out of 6: the shortcut was
right about which files were there.

35
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:35,563
Then I gave both a wrong
file, deliberately,

36
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:38,263
on a scratch copy that never
went near the repository.

37
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:41,903
In the NVDA price file I
switched two digits around.

38
00:01:42,513 --> 00:01:47,983
504.5625 became 540.5625.
It is still a number.

39
00:01:48,133 --> 00:01:49,503
It is still valid JSON.

40
00:01:49,873 --> 00:01:52,163
It is the kind of slip a
person makes at a keyboard.

41
00:01:53,167 --> 00:01:54,737
Here is what the old route did.

42
00:01:55,007 --> 00:01:57,517
Step one checks the files against
the manifest, and it stopped.

43
00:01:57,947 --> 00:02:00,997
Its message says: fixture changed
since the manifest froze it.

44
00:02:01,017 --> 00:02:05,017
But step three, run on the same folder,
said pass: 5 promoted, none rejected.

45
00:02:05,267 --> 00:02:10,257
And step five priced the
portfolio at $66,898.13.

46
00:02:10,277 --> 00:02:14,177
The committed run says $65,098.13.

47
00:02:14,197 --> 00:02:15,697
That is $1,800 more,

48
00:02:16,147 --> 00:02:21,497
from two switched digits, and a
return of 24.35% instead of 21%.

49
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:25,143
I want to be careful
here. Step one did stop.

50
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:28,023
What step three, on its own, has
no way to know is that it did.

51
00:02:28,153 --> 00:02:29,603
Each step is a separate command,

52
00:02:29,623 --> 00:02:32,543
and the stop was only a message
in a file until someone read it.

53
00:02:32,713 --> 00:02:34,723
The stand-in never had
to ask the question,

54
00:02:34,743 --> 00:02:37,603
because I had put the question
somewhere else. Nobody was wrong.

55
00:02:37,753 --> 00:02:41,703
It was my sentence, since step two is
still open, doing exactly what it said.

56
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,390
Now the same folder, through the
real step two. Four files taken in.

57
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:49,810
One turned away, NVDA, with
its reason written beside it:

58
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:51,970
it differs from the hash
recorded in the manifest.

59
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:54,070
The same wrong file,
through a different door,

60
00:02:54,090 --> 00:02:56,850
and this time it is stopped by
the step whose job is to ask.

61
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:00,310
Wiring step three to read what
step two kept is the next change.

62
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:01,470
I have not made it.

63
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,370
So what did the stand-in
skip? Two questions.

64
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:06,250
Is this file one that was ever recorded?

65
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:08,390
And is it the same file that was recorded?

66
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,390
Step three asked whether a file parsed,

67
00:03:10,410 --> 00:03:12,410
named one of my five
tickers, and had a price.

68
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:15,510
It never asked whether it was
the very file I had recorded.

69
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:17,750
A hash can only say a file is unchanged.

70
00:03:17,999 --> 00:03:19,550
It cannot say the file is right.

71
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:21,850
But unchanged was
exactly what was missing.

72
00:03:22,833 --> 00:03:25,523
There is a second stand-in,
and I have not paid it back.

73
00:03:25,913 --> 00:03:29,283
Step five still reads step three's
output, and the recipe still says,

74
00:03:29,303 --> 00:03:32,683
since step four is still open,
even though step four now exists.

75
00:03:33,253 --> 00:03:36,663
I have not compared the two yet, so I
will not say anything about it here,

76
00:03:36,683 --> 00:03:38,203
except that I have it written down.

77
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:41,510
If you build out of order,
you have stand-ins too.

78
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:43,930
Search your own notes for
the words since and until.

79
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,670
For each one, write down what
it replaced and what it skipped.

80
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:48,710
Then, when the real step arrives,

81
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:51,670
hand the stand-in and the real
step one deliberately wrong input,

82
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:52,890
and read where they disagree.

83
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,790
That one wrong file told me
more than six clean sets.

84
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:58,250
Here is what I did not check.

85
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,470
This was sample mode,
with invented prices:

86
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,990
one swapped pair, in
one file, in one recipe.

87
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:05,330
Step one does catch it
when it is run and read;

88
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,470
my claim is only about
step three on its own.

89
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:10,870
No live call was made. The
wrong file was a scratch copy,

90
00:04:10,890 --> 00:04:14,470
so none of it is in the repository,
and the recipe's own text is unedited,

91
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:16,370
so it still says step two is open.

92
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:19,250
Step two was the last one I built.

93
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:21,470
It was the one the others
were standing in for.

94
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:25,290
A stand-in is a loan, and this was
the week I started paying it back.
